---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import W from "../data/work.json";

const FAM = { blazevic: "Blažević", zubrinic: "Žubrinić", papic: "Papić",
              prpic: "Prpić", kalanj: "Kalanj", boras: "Boras",
              sestan: "Šestan", vukelic: "Vukelić" };
const reg = W.rows.filter((r) => r.from === "register");
const tree = W.rows.filter((r) => r.from === "tree");
const byFam = Object.entries(
  W.rows.reduce((a, r) => (r.family ? ((a[r.family] = (a[r.family] || 0) + 1), a) : a), {})
).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
---
<Base title="What They Did"
  eyebrow={`${W.rows.length} occupations — ${W.fromRegister} read off registers, ${W.fromTree} carried by the tree`}
  dek="The family tree records a trade for sixteen people out of twelve hundred. The registers record one for almost everybody, in a column this archive had been reading past.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Both the Latin and the Croatian parish forms carry a
    <em>conditio</em> or <em>stalež</em> column, and the priest filled it in — for the parents, for
    the godparents, and for the witnesses. It is the only place in these documents where a person is
    described by something other than their name and their kin.</p>
    <p>This archive had been reading past it. Where an occupation surfaced it went into prose on one
    person's page and nowhere else, which meant it could not be counted or compared. <strong>The
    comparison is the point.</strong></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Two families, two worlds, twenty miles apart</h2>
    <div class="grid two">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The Žubrinići hold offices</h3>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li><strong>Confiniarius</strong> — a man of the <a href={u("/frontier")}>Military
            Frontier</a>, Šumećica N° 24, 1834</li>
          <li><strong>Confiniarius ex Pago Dubrava N° 29</strong> — and so is
            <strong>Marcus Orešković</strong>, whose daughter married into this line. Both sides of
            that marriage are Frontier households.</li>
          <li><strong>Vigiliarum Magister pensionatus</strong> — master of the watch, pensioned</li>
          <li><strong>Silvarum Custos</strong> — forest warden, Šumećica</li>
          <li><strong>Custos Carcerum</strong> — keeper of the prison, Otočac, 1845</li>
          <li><strong>Forest Ranger</strong> — Otočac, the 1837 generation</li>
          <li><strong>kontrolor gradske daće</strong> — municipal excise controller, Senj, 1910</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Six salaried posts, four of them in a single parish
        register, three of them in <em>one entry</em>. These are people the state paid.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The Blaževići carry things</h3>
        <ul class="plain">
          <li><strong>nadničar</strong> — day labourer. Toma Blažević, Senj, at his death in 1907</li>
          <li><strong>seljaci</strong> — peasants. Toma and Tereza at Smokvica 114, 1884</li>
          <li><strong>selj.</strong> — the same, Jurko and Tereza, 1895</li>
          <li><strong>težak</strong> — labourer. Josip Papić, Senj, 1891</li>
          <li><strong>trhonoša, bremenar, gostioničar</strong> — porter, burden-carrier, innkeeper</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">No office, no pension, no rank. The
        <a href={u("/papic")}>Papići</a> carry sacks in the port; the Blaževići work by the day.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem">These families are <strong>twenty miles apart</strong> and they
    married each other. <a href={u("/hedviga")}>Hedviga</a> descends from both — from a household
    that held the keys to the Otočac gaol and from a household whose head was entered, at his death,
    as a man who worked by the day. <a href={u("/frontier")}>The Frontier →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Read off the registers</h2>
    <p class="lede">Each row cites the entry it came from. Nothing here is inferred, and where a
    word is ambiguous it is given as written rather than translated away.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>As written</th><th>In English</th><th>Where</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{reg.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{r.who}</td>
          <td><strong>{r.what}</strong></td>
          <td class="dim">{r.english}</td>
          <td class="dim">{r.place}{r.year ? " · " + r.year : ""}</td>
          <td class="dek" style="font-size:.86em">{r.source}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And the {tree.length} the tree carries</h2>
    <p class="lede">Sixteen people out of {W.published.toLocaleString()}. Mostly twentieth-century,
    mostly from family memory rather than a document — and worth showing beside the register rows so
    the difference in kind is visible.</p>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>Occupation</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{tree.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{r.slug ? <a href={u("/people/" + r.slug)}>{r.who}</a> : r.who}</td>
          <td>{r.what}</td>
          <td class="dim">{r.place || "—"}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What this is not, yet</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>It is not a survey.</strong> Thirty-eight records against
      {" "}{W.published.toLocaleString()} people. Every one had to be read off a scan by hand, and
      <a href={u("/searched")}>twenty-seven register pages this archive already holds</a> have not
      been read for this column at all.</li>
      <li><strong>It is the form of the register, not its language.</strong> This was assumed the
      other way round and checked on 13 September 2026. The <strong>ruled tabular</strong> books
      carry a <em>conditio</em> column — Otočac 1834, 1835 and 1845 give a trade or a rank to
      <em>every parent and every godparent</em>, which is why almost everything above comes from
      three pages. The <strong>prose</strong> books have no column at all: Karlobag 1754–1781 runs
      as continuous Latin sentences, and <strong>six of the eight Latin documents this archive holds
      are those</strong>. Reading them for occupations is wasted effort. Croatian tabular books sit
      in between — Novi Vinodolski names the parents' trade but usually not the godparents', and
      Sv. Jakov Krmpote mostly writes <em>seljaci</em> and stops.</li>
      <li><strong>A woman is usually described by her husband.</strong> <em>Mulier Custodis
      Carcerum</em> — the prison keeper's wife — names an office and does not name her trade or, in
      that entry, even her husband. The column records women through men, and saying so is part of
      reading it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a class="btn" href={u("/frontier")}>The Frontier →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/zubrinic")}>Žubrinić →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/searched")}>What has been searched →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
